#{[17592186107077 "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =({a}_{1}\\mathbf{i}+{a}_{2}\\mathbf{j}+{a}_{3}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}-{a}_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}+{a}_{3}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121083 "If a surface is parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ then the **fundamental vector product** of the surface is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v = \n\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂u} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn particular, if the fundamental vector product of a surface is nonzero at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ then it defines a normal vector to the surface at this point.\n\nLet's find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle \\sqrt{2}\\cos{u}, \\: \\sqrt{2}\\sin{u}, \\: v \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186130394 "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t), \\qquad E = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'(t) =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}{\\lambda }_{1}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}{\\lambda }_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\mathbf{u}(t).\n$$\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$"] [17592186135034 "The standard inner product of $\\Bbb R^n,$ i.e. the dot product, does not meet the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFor example, if $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is a valid inner product on $\\Bbb C^n,$ then for any vector $\\mathbf a \\in \\Bbb C^n,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathbf a, \\, \\mathbf a\\rangle \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nBut for many vectors in $\\Bbb C^n,$ such as $\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ in $\\Bbb C^2,$ the dot product fails to satisfy this criterion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThankfully, there is an elegant way to generalize the dot product to satisfy the desired criterion on $\\Bbb C^n.$ We can just take the complex conjugate of each element in the right vector before carrying out the dot product.\n\nMore formally, for two vectors $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in \\mathbb C^n$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{y} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {y}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nwe define the **standard Hermitian inner product** as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{i}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{y_i}$ is the complex conjugate of $y_i.$\n\nUsing the standard Hermitian inner product instead of the dot product, our desired criterion is satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}⟩ & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}\\,}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,0\\,} \\\\  & =\\text{i}\\cdot (-\\text{i})+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ge 0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be verified that the Hermitian inner product satisfies *all* the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFurthermore, since the complex conjugate leaves real numbers unchanged, the Hermitian inner product simplifies to the dot product on $\\Bbb R^n.$ In other words, the dot product is just a special case of the Hermitian inner product!"] [17592186135049 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\:\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = 0 \\\\ ( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} \\\\ {k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t=\n{\\color{red}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}| \\\\ \\mathbf{x} \\\\ |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nand $\\mathbf{k}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!"] [17592186135059 "Let's consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIt is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. \n\nLet's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & - 4 \\\\ 2 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{ A}\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{ \\mathbf{x}}\n=\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{ \\mathbf{b}}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in vectors $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nThe smaller the value of $\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert,$ the better our approximation $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ will be. \n\nIn terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\\mathbf{b}.$\nTherefore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nIt can be shown that ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf b$ by $A^T.$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \\! A)^{-1}A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{99}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{33} \\\\ -\\frac{38}{33}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.\n\n**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \\! A\\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem."] [17592186158440 "We’ve seen how both Euclidean and Manhattan distances can be written in terms of vector norms. These are actually special cases of a more general formula known as the **Minkowski distance**.\n\nGiven a fixed value $p \\ge 1$, the Minkowski distance between two points $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ in $N$-dimensional space is defined as\n\n$$\nd_p(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}) = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |a_i - b_i|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **Minkowski norm** is given by\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|_p = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $p$ determines how differences in individual coordinates contribute to the total distance:\n\n- **$p=1$** (Manhattan): All differences are summed equally.\n\n- **$p=2$** (Euclidean): Larger differences have more weight due to squaring.\n\n- **$p \\to \\infty$**: The distance is determined entirely by the single largest coordinate difference.\n\nLet’s compute an example to see how this works in practice.\n\nSuppose $\\mathbf{a} = [1,\\ 2,\\ 3]^T$ and $\\mathbf{b} = [4,\\ 0,\\ 6]^T$. Let's compute their Minkowski distance using $p = 5$.\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{5}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={(|1-4{|}^{5}+|2-0{|}^{5}+|3-6{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={(|-3{|}^{5}+{2}^{5}+|-3{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={(243+32+243)}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={518}^{1/5} \\\\  & ≈3.490.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's try a few examples to build intuition."] [17592186158466 "Consider a Jordan matrix formed by the direct sum of blocks $J_{n_1}(\\lambda_1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} \\cdots {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_{n_k}(\\lambda_k)$ and a function $f.$ By applying the rule for diagonal matrices to this block diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,{J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})) & =f(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & {J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}))\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to compute functions of any matrix in Jordan Normal Form by applying the function to each Jordan block individually.\n\nFor example, suppose $J = J_2(2) \\oplus J_1(3).$ Then, applying a function $f,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(J)\n= f(J_2(2) \\oplus J_1(3))\n= f(J_2(2)) \\oplus f(J_1(3))\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({J}_{1}(3))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186158609 "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} )\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\n\\qquad \n\n\\mathbf b\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_1 \\approx\n\\delta u \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_2 \\approx\n\\delta v \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  v}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  v}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)\n\\approx\n\\left|\n\\det {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{s}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{s}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\right|.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{|det\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186163310 "The table below describes the number of classes taken per semester and the average final grade in a sample of four college students.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Number of Courses</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Average Grade</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m,$ is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 80\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 85\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 75\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_4 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 72\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 312\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the average number of courses is $4,$ and the average final grade is $78.$"] [17592186163312 "Recall that if the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ Furthermore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ that's closest to $\\mathbf b.$ \n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ Therefore, the error vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nA similar property holds for weighted least-squares solutions:\n\n*If the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf x=W\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then\n$A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $w_i$'s are the weights from the main diagonal of the weight matrix $W.$* \n\nWe previously considered the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!Ax=W\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\nW = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show (using the usual techniques) that the unique weighted least-squares solution is given by \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 6\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nComputing our error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} = A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε & =A\\hat{x}-b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ denote the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad  \\mathbf a_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad  \\mathbf a_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNotice that the error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is *not* orthogonal to the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{1} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{2} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we check the orthogonality with respect to the *weighted* dot product \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i = 4x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + x_3y_3,\n\\quad\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⟨ε,{a}_{1}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {⟨ε,{a}_{2}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product. We can write this as\n\n$$\nA{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\perp_W \\textrm{Col}(A).\n$$\n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection with respect to $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W$ of the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ \n\nFurthermore, the weighted least-squares problem with weight matrix $W$ is equivalent to finding a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert_W\n$$\n\nis as small as possible."] [17592186163319 "The **leading principal minors** of an $n\\times n$ matrix are the $n$ determinants formed by the first $r$ rows and first $r$ columns of $A$ for $r = 1,2,3,\\ldots, n.$ The leading principal minors of $A$ are denoted $\\Delta_i.$\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first leading principal minors $\\Delta_1$ equals $a_{11},$ and\n\n- the $n$th leading principal minors $\\Delta_n$ equals $\\det(A).$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & - 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 8\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is a $3\\times3$ matrix, it has $3$ leading principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of $A:$ \n$$\n{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{1} = \\left| \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{11}\\end{array} \\right| = \\left| \\begin{array}{c}1\\end{array} \\right| = 1\n$$\n\n- The second leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first $2$ rows and first $2$ columns of $A:$ \n$$\n{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{2} = ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{array} ∣ = 3\n$$\n\n- The third leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first $3$ rows and first $3$ columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{3} = ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & - 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 8\\end{array} ∣ = 60\n$$"] [17592186163321 "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 80\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 85\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 75\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_4 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 72\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe also found the sample mean vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf m = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 78\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nOne thing we can do with this data is to check how much each observation differs from the mean values. To do that, we compute the difference between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={x}_{4}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\nB =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}0 & - 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & - 3 & - 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X.$ It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{B} & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186168006 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.32$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)"] [17592186173252 "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180758 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186185333 "One use for finding the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace is to find the shortest distance between the vector and the subspace.\n\nGiven a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and a subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$** is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d10fd1778529183deb6912cebe9d2b7ad405091fe21a261c464d3d22c6d0860b.png)\n\nLet's find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have \n$$\nA\n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]} \n={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 1 \\\\ - 1 & - 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad A^T={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & - 1 \\\\ 2 & - 1 & - 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the distance formula, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-2))^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Recall that the vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**"] [17592186199473 "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186199488 "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where $\\mathbf{x} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]},$ the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\n𝒇'(\\mathbf x)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  x}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  y}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ z-f(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$"] [17592186199502 "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overbrace{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}a \\\\ b\\end{array} \\right]},$ then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n$$\n𝒇'(\\mathbf a) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{f}_{x} ( a , b ) & {f}_{y} ( a , b )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f(a)+\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n𝒇''(\\mathbf a) =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{f}_{x x} ( \\mathbf{a} ) & {f}_{x y} ( \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {f}_{y x} ( \\mathbf{a} ) & {f}_{y y} ( \\mathbf{a} )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x-a & y-b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}x & y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overbrace{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}}+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$"] [17592186209961 "In machine learning and linear algebra, it’s often convenient to express the Hadamard product of two vectors using matrix multiplication. If $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\odot \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v}) \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{u}) \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix product\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nwe notice that the left matrix is diagonal. So, we can write it as a Hadamard product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= diag(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's see a concrete example of expressing a Hadamard product as a matrix product in the next slide."] [17592186222500 "Suppose we have a system of ODEs such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwith a nonzero equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ satisfying\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nInstead of analyzing the system near a *nonzero* equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ we can introduce *shifted coordinates* so that the equilibrium becomes the origin.\nWe define the **deviation vector**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nso that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nThis transformed system has several advantages: shifting the equilibrium to the origin makes the system easier to analyze without altering its behavior.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 2 \\\\ - 5 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\mathbf{x}\n+\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, -1]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} -\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}\n= \\mathbf{u} +\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{u}'.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}' =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 & 2 \\\\ - 5 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nNotice that for linear systems, shifting the equilibrium to the origin just removes the constant term."]}
